## Aim

Project documents now have two kinds of readers. People read them in an editor or a preview, and AI agents read them as context for their work. Today the author has no say in what the agent sees, and cannot easily check it. Semantic Markdown lets the author mark one source for both audiences and shows them the agent's view.

The research question is whether this works for people and for agents. A smaller agent context is only useful if the agent still gets the task right, and audience markup is only useful if authors can apply it and predict its effect. The paper so far measures text exposure only. This plan adds the evidence a CHI, IUI or UIST paper needs.

## Research questions

| ID | Question | Answered by | Primary outcome |
|---|---|---|---|
| RQ0 | How do developers share project documents with AI agents today, and what goes wrong? | Study 0 | Themes and design requirements |
| RQ1 | Does an author-controlled view keep what an agent needs to do a task correctly, compared with plain Markdown and ordinary search? | Study 1 | Task correctness and constraint violations |
| RQ2 | Can authors predict what an agent will see in a document they marked? | Study 2 | Accuracy of predicted agent visibility |
| RQ3 | Does showing authors the agent's view, with what it left out, help them find and fix omissions? | Study 2 | Seeded omissions found and repaired |
| RQ4 | Does the approach change how well people oversee an agent that works from the document? | Study 3 (later) | Agent errors caught, trust calibration |

## Contributions we aim to claim

Each claim is made only once the study behind it has run.

1. Design requirements for documents with two audiences, grounded in practice (Study 0).
2. The system, with author-controlled audience markup, semantic blocks, persistent agent instructions and an inspector that shows the agent's view and its omissions.
3. Evidence on when selective agent views keep task correctness and when they lose it, including failure cases (Study 1).
4. Evidence on whether authors can manage two audiences and whether the inspector helps (Study 2).

## Studies

| Study | Method | Participants | Depends on | Document |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 Formative | Semi-structured interviews with an artifact walk-through | 8 to 12 developers and technical writers | Ethics approval | [Study 0](study-0-formative.smd) |
| 1 Agent evaluation | Controlled comparison over an annotated corpus, repeated model runs | None (models only) | Corpus, harness, review fixes C01 and C02 | [Study 1](study-1-agent-evaluation.smd) |
| 2 Authoring | Within-subjects lab study with think-aloud | 12 to 20 developers | Omission panel (C10), study logging, ethics approval | [Study 2](study-2-authoring.smd) |
| 3 Collaboration | People supervise an agent working from a document | 16 to 24 | Studies 1 and 2 | Not drafted yet |

## Evidence so far {agent=skip}

The current manuscript is a descriptive systems paper. It reports character and token counts on one literature document and an analysis of the project's net-tokens benchmark. It does not measure comprehension, accuracy or cost.

An earlier informal trial had a coding agent build a small API from one requirements document. Every condition passed every check, and the document was under 1% of the tokens of a run. One long build task per document cannot separate the conditions. Study 1 is designed around that lesson, with many short tasks, harder documents and smaller models.

The implementation review of 2026-11-05 found that brief views dropped agent instructions nested in `:::details` (C01) and that the benchmark resolved includes from the wrong folder (C02). Both are fixed on the `fix/implementation-review` branch. Any measurement taken before that fix must say so.

## Order of work

:::steps
1. **Settle the venue and ethics route.** The venue fixes page limits and whether a systems paper without a user study can pass. Ethics approval is needed before Study 0 or Study 2 recruits anyone.
2. **Run Study 0** while the Study 1 corpus is being built. Revise the design requirements from its themes.
3. **Build and pilot Study 1.** Preregister it, then run the confirmatory study.
4. **Build the omission panel and study logging**, pilot Study 2 with three people, preregister, then run it.
5. **Rewrite the paper** around the questions above, under the Semantic Markdown name, keeping older measurements labelled with their version.
:::

## Venues

| Evidence in hand | Fit |
|---|---|
| Study 1 and a pilot of Study 2 | CHI Late-Breaking Work, IUI short paper |
| Studies 0, 1 and 2 | CHI or IUI full paper |
| A strong system with Studies 1 and 2 | UIST |
| Study 3 | CSCW |
| Study 1 alone | An ACL or EMNLP workshop or findings track |

## Ethics and data

Studies 0, 2 and 3 involve people and need approval from the author's institution before recruitment. Participants give informed consent, can withdraw at any time, and are paid for their time if the institution allows. Recordings and logs are stored encrypted and deleted after transcription and analysis. Quotes are anonymised. Participants never bring confidential documents. Study 0 asks them to describe or redact, and Studies 2 and 3 use prepared documents.

Study 1 sends only synthetic or permissively licensed documents to model providers. The materials and analysis code are released. AI assistance in drafting materials is disclosed in the paper.

## Risks

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=high owner=@roshanmadhushanka} Ceiling effect in the agent evaluation
Strong models pass well-written plain Markdown tasks anyway, as the earlier trial showed. Mitigation is many short tasks, long and messy documents, superseded decisions placed where they look authoritative, and at least one smaller model.
:::

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=medium owner=@roshanmadhushanka} The tool's author marks the documents
How a document is marked decides what the agent sees, and the author knows the tool best. Mitigation is a written marking guide, a second person marking a subset, and reporting results separately for author-marked and independently marked documents.
:::

:::risk{impact=medium likelihood=medium owner=@roshanmadhushanka} Too few participants
Developers who use AI agents are busy. Mitigation is short remote sessions, payment, recruitment through several channels, and a design that is within-subjects so 12 people can show large effects.
:::

:::risk{impact=medium likelihood=high owner=@roshanmadhushanka} Models change during the study
Model versions change behaviour between runs. Mitigation is pinning model IDs, running all conditions of one model in the same window, and recording versions and dates.
:::

## Open questions

:::question Which venue and deadline?
The author decides the target venue and track. It sets the scope of the first submission. Until then the plan works toward CHI Late-Breaking Work as the first milestone.
:::

:::question Which ethics office and what review level?
The author confirms the institution, the review route and whether interviews count as minimal risk. No participant is contacted before approval.
:::

:::question Model budget and providers for Study 1
The author decides which providers and how much spend Study 1 can use. Until then the protocol assumes three models from at least two providers and sizes the main run after the pilot.
:::

:::decision{status=proposed date=2026-11-05 owner=@roshanmadhushanka} Run the agent evaluation before recruiting people
Study 1 needs no participants or approval and decides whether the selective views keep task correctness. If they don't, the system changes before Study 2 tests it with people.
:::

:::agent Research constraints
- These are proposals. Do not present any study as run until its results exist in this repository.
- Keep reproduced results, pilot results and planned studies apart in every document and in the paper.
- Use Semantic Markdown as the product name. Measurements from 2.6.0 or earlier keep their original version label.
- Never contact participants, send study materials or submit anything to a venue or ethics office on your own.
- Synthetic or licensed documents only for anything sent to a model provider.
:::
